course: dictionary
lesson: 8631
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can name a thing and also show an action. Scull is one of them. A scull is one of a pair of oars, the long poles that push a boat through the water. To scull is to row a boat with them.
explain: Read the words around it. «They sculled home to their own cosy riverside parlour, to the Rat's great joy.» Sculled tells you what they did, so it is an action word here: they rowed the boat home.
explain: Now look for a thing instead. In «single sculls», scull names the oars that one rower uses. Ask yourself: is the word showing someone doing something, or naming something? That question tells you which meaning to use.
fact: A scull can be a noun: one of a pair of oars, or a light, narrow racing boat.
fact: To scull can be a verb: to row a boat with a pair of sculls, or with one oar at the back of the boat.
fact: «Scull a boat» means row a boat with sculls.
fact: «Shipped his sculls» means pulled the oars into the boat.
fact: In sculling, one rower holds one scull in each hand. The rower faces backwards, because pulling the oars towards you is the strongest way to row.
exercise: «They sculled home to their own cosy riverside parlour.» What does sculled mean here? | A kind of song | To eat a big dinner | *To row a boat with sculls | To tie a boat to a post
exercise: In the race called «single sculls», what are the sculls? | Sails on a boat | Long pieces of rope | A kind of hat | *Oars that one rower uses
exercise: «Ratty sculls his little boat on the river.» Is scull a doing word or a naming word here? | Describing word | Joining word | *Doing word, a verb | Naming word
test: «Mole grabbed the sculls to show off.» What are the sculls in this sentence? | *Oars, the poles used to row | A cup of tea | A grassy hill | A type of bird
test: Which meaning is right when scull is a verb? | *To row a boat with a pair of sculls or one oar at the back | To shout loudly across a river | To stand still in the water | To tie a boat to a post
exam: Why do sculling rowers face backwards? | *Pulling the oars towards them is the strongest way to row | Their seats only face that way | They like the view of the shore | They are copying the Mole
